rigen's interpretation."},{"id":"92992dbda79c","v":"anf.cyprian.ad_demetrianum","w":"cyprian.ad_demetrianum","a":"cyprian_von_karthago","s":2,"p":[19,19],"r":[3,17],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. iii. 17."},{"id":"d9cb225ab142","v":"anf.cyprian.epistulae","w":"cyprian.epistulae","a":"cyprian_von_karthago","s":54,"p":[5,5],"r":[2,5],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. ii. 5."},{"id":"530b5ee66ae0","v":"npnf.hieronymus.epistulae","w":"hieronymus.epistulae","a":"hieronymus","s":47,"p":[29,29],"r":[1,8],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"i.e. the Huns have taken the place of the Chaldaeans described in Hab. 1:8, LXX"},{"id":"326f93b2038b","v":"npnf.leo.tractatus","w":"leo.tractatus","a":"leo_der_grosse","s":3,"p":[1,1],"r":[3,2],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. iii. 2 (LXX.)."},{"id":"d54c352a1358","v":"npnf.gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","w":"gregor_gr.regula_pastoralis","a":"gregor_der_grosse","s":33,"p":[3,3],"r":[2,6],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. ii. 6"},{"id":"ead0f8b5fefb","v":"npnf.kyrill_jer.catecheses","w":"kyrill_jer.catecheses","a":"cyrillus_von_jerusalem","s":13,"p":[23,23],"r":[3,3],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. iii. 3. Cyril interprets the word Θαιμάν (Heb. wm/yt@'"},{"id":"d55e7cc4913e","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":2,"p":[61,61],"r":[1,2],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. i. 2 et seq."},{"id":"ab592e0ccd89","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":9,"p":[11,11],"r":[2,16],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. ii. 16."},{"id":"91b9a4e4bd4d","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":9,"p":[11,11],"r":[1,5],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. i. 5; Acts xiii. 41."},{"id":"05b2e7621919","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":24,"p":[7,7],"r":[3,13],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. iii. 13."},{"id":"3c1327f4d066","v":"npnf.gregor_naz.orationes","w":"gregor_naz.orationes","a":"gregor_von_nazianz","s":26,"p":[3,3],"r":[2,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Hab. ii. 1."},{"id":"e8f3ee8d484f","v":"npnf.eusebius.historia_ecclesiastica","w":"eusebius.historia_ecclesiastica","a":"eusebius_von_caesarea","s":8,"p":[236,236],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"This is a glaring instance of uncritical credulity on Eusebius' part, and yet even Crusè can say: \"Perhaps some might smile at the supposed credulity of our author, but the miracle in this account was not greater than the malignity, and if man can perform miracles of vice, we can scarcely wonder if Providence should present, at least, miracles of admonition.\" Cureton more sensibly remarks: \"This, which doubtless was produced by natural causes, seemed miraculous to Eusebius, more especially if he looked upon it as fulfilling a prophecy of our Lord-Luke xix. 40: `I tell you, that if these should hold their peace, the stones would immediately cry out. 0' See also Hab. ii. 11.\""},{"id":"3270641431f8","v":"npnf.eusebius.historia_ecclesiastica","w":"eusebius.historia_ecclesiastica","a":"eusebius_von_caesarea","s":9,"p":[84,84],"r":[2,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"This is a glaring instance of uncritical credulity on Eusebius' part, and yet even Crusè can say: \"Perhaps some might smile at the supposed credulity of our author, but the miracle in this account was not greater than the malignity, and if man can perform miracles of vice, we can scarcely wonder if Providence should present, at least, miracles of admonition.\" Cureton more sensibly remarks: \"This, which doubtless was produced by natural causes, see